window.docContents[156].resolve({"/Calculational-proofs/#Kernel-Hom___-Tactics-for-Kernel-Categorical-Reasoning--Calculational-proofs--Basu___s-theorem":{"contents":"As an example, we formalize Theorem 15.8 of , which is the main ingredient of the classical Basu theorem: a complete sufficient statistic is independent of any ancillary statistic, for every value of the parameter. A statistical model is a Markov kernel p : Kernel Θ X, and almost sure equality, sufficiency, completeness and ancillarity are all defined by equalities of kernels:\n\n\n\nThe proof consists of two calculations. The first one shows that a ∘ₖ α and ψ ∘ₖ discard V agree after s ∘ₖ p, where α witnesses the sufficiency of s and ψ the ancillarity of a:\n\n\n\nThe visible rewrites are the sufficiency equation hα and the ancillarity equation hψ. The first line marginalizes the right-hand side of the sufficiency equation, so that hα can be applied. After each rewrite, kernel_disch proves the remaining equality.\n\nThe second calculation uses the completeness of s ∘ₖ p on the first one:\n\n\n\nThe visible rewrites are the sufficiency equation hα, the completeness of s ∘ₖ p (h) and the ancillarity equation hψ. The last calculation step before the ancillarity is the factorization of Theorem 15.8 of , where the joint distribution of s and a is the product of s ∘ₖ p and of the distribution ψ.\n\n","context":"Kernel-Hom: Tactics for Kernel Categorical Reasoning\u0009Calculational proofs","header":"2.1. Basu's theorem","id":"/Calculational-proofs/#Kernel-Hom___-Tactics-for-Kernel-Categorical-Reasoning--Calculational-proofs--Basu___s-theorem"}});